1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,012
Wednesday.

2
00:00:01,062 --> 00:00:01,862
Ines.

3
00:00:05,801 --> 00:00:10,289
The problem: even a warning at apply
time arrives after the code is merged.

4
00:00:10,339 --> 00:00:13,859
This part shows the same check
running in the pull request,

5
00:00:13,909 --> 00:00:16,340
with exit codes the pipeline understands.

6
00:00:17,505 --> 00:00:18,305
Wednesday.

7
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:21,885
Even a warning at apply time
arrives after the code is merged.

8
00:00:21,935 --> 00:00:26,745
Ines wants the answer in the pull request,
before anything reaches the cluster.

9
00:00:27,918 --> 00:00:32,293
She adds this script, taken from the
Tyk documentation, to her pipeline.

10
00:00:32,343 --> 00:00:37,456
It sends the spec to the Dashboard's sandbox
endpoint and asks for a verdict against

11
00:00:37,506 --> 00:00:38,624
the payments rules.

12
00:00:38,674 --> 00:00:40,407
Nothing is saved or deployed.

13
00:00:41,552 --> 00:00:46,792
If any gating rule fails, the script exits
with code two, which fails the build.

14
00:00:46,842 --> 00:00:49,040
Warnings on their own let it pass.

15
00:00:50,212 --> 00:00:53,271
Here it runs against Monday's
version of her spec.

16
00:00:53,321 --> 00:00:57,312
It finds the same errors Ines read
on Tuesday, each with its fix,

17
00:00:57,362 --> 00:00:58,805
and exits with code two.

18
00:00:58,855 --> 00:01:03,281
The pull request would have failed
before Rob even knew the API existed.

19
00:01:04,421 --> 00:01:08,723
And against the fixed version:
zero errors, exit code zero.

20
00:01:08,773 --> 00:01:10,493
The pipeline carries on.

21
00:01:11,654 --> 00:01:12,547
The pull request,

22
00:01:12,597 --> 00:01:16,594
the cluster and the Dashboard all use
the same rules and the same engine.

23
00:01:16,644 --> 00:01:18,812
The answer is the same wherever you ask,

24
00:01:18,862 --> 00:01:21,972
and that is what makes it safe
to move the check earlier.
